  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nMostly sunny skies, relatively low humidity values, and \nseasonably warm temperatures in the upper 70s to lower 80s are \nmaking for a beautiful Labor Day in the Great Lakes thanks to a \nlarge high pressure system centered over southern Ontario. \nTonight looks similarly pleasant with overnight lows around 60, \nlow humidity values, and light winds. Suppose a few patches of \nground fog cannot be ruled out toward day break near river \nvalleys and farm fields, but will withhold mention in the \nforecast for now. \n\nTomorrow, the semi-persistent upper-level ridge over the \ncentral United States will begin to flatten as an upper-level \ntrough and associated surface low lift into central lower \nCanada. An increase in south to southwesterly winds will advect \na warmer airmass into the region with highs poised to rise into \nthe mid to upper 80s. A lake breeze should develop by early \nafternoon and stall inland with cooler temperatures along the \nlakeshore. \n\nTomorrow afternoon and evening, showers and thunderstorms will \ndevelop in a low-level confluence axis across Iowa and \nWisconsin. With time, the activity will tend to outrun any \nupper-level ascent leading to a gradual weakening trend while \napproaching and especially crossing the Mississippi River. Can \nnevertheless still envision scattered showers and perhaps a \nstorm moving across the area overnight through Wednesday \nmorning, but will cap PoPs at 60% to signal a lower coverage/QPF\nevent.\n\nAfter morning showers, Wednesday afternoon is looking \nrelatively quiet. Highs will depend on how fast clouds clear and\nshould range from the low to mid 80s. A few showers and storms \nmay attempt to develop across the south and east portions of our\nforecast area depending on where any residual front stalls. \n\nThursday and Friday look pleasant as a surface high pressure \nsystem work into the Great Lakes region. Opted to cut NBM \ntemperatures by several degrees each day to feature highs \ngenerally in the mid to upper 70s both days. Ensemble guidance \nthen suggests warming temperatures by the weekend accompanied by\nthe next opportunity for rain. \n\nBorchardt",
